173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
175:**The Core rows are a provisional approval (stage A2a).** They bind the design without freezing it. Stage A2b formalizes them in a proposed `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` and proves the four design theorems; any row the formalization changes returns to the owner; stage A2i integrates the parts (2026-11-06); the owner freezes Core at A2c (row Q9.19 as revised). Until a rule is written in Lean, the answer below governs over any chapter text that differs.
179:- **OD45 and OD87** (rows Q5.1, Q5.4): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked against the callee; there is no capability inference and no inference solver. OD46 (type arguments from checked argument types) is unchanged.
180:- **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
181:- **TRAIT-1@1** (row Q2.8): two impls with equal heads may coexist only when Copy/Destroy disjointness (OD28) makes their domains disjoint, as in `impl<T: Copy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>` beside `impl<T: Destroy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>`. No other coherence or orphan rule changes, and no disjunctive bound syntax is added.
182:- **The generated-chapter requirement** (rows Q9.15, P3): Lean is the specification, in one main normative file with doc comments and examples; no prose is generated from it. This confirms the [2026-11-04 decision](#decided-project-lean-is-the-spec). The "one main normative file" wording is revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): one authoritative specification library in cohesive modules, with `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` as its public import.
183:- **Authority rules 13, 5 and 7** (row Q7.11): `Console` joins the reserved capability set, for operations on the `StdIn`, `StdOut` and `StdErr` grants; rule 5's operational list and rule 7's primitive table include it. `Device` stays for device grants.
184:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
185:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
186:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q9-1-reads"></a>**Q9.1, read forms** (owner-confirmed, 2026-11-05): `copy x` is allowed only when `x` has a Copy type and `move x` only when it has a non-Copy (linear) type. Each read has one form; `move` of a Copy value and `copy` of a linear value are both refused.
188:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-new-rules"></a>**New rules** (P4): duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl are refused, like duplicate fields (bug 313); the rule gets its ID and diagnostic when it is written in Lean.
192:- **Q1.1** What is an allocator brand? — Separate erased `Brand` kind.
193:- **Q1.2** How are fresh brands introduced, including loop resets? — Generative binders; existential loop state.
194:- **Q1.3** How are packages formed and opened? — Explicit pack; lexical unpack; repackage escapes.
195:- **Q1.4** How are brands compared and supplied? — Nominal brand equality; explicit brand arguments.
196:- **Q1.5** How are allocator dependencies tracked across calls? — Structural dependencies; checked interface summaries.
197:- **Q1.6** Who owns a child allocator’s backing? — Child owns backing; parent brand exposed in type. *Revises or adds: Clarify/extend allocator interface.*
198:- **Q1.7** Where does release information live? — Stable release state and raw context; no safe references.
199:- **Q1.8** What if creating allocator state fails? — Fallible allocator construction conserving surviving inputs.
200:- **Q1.9** What do allocation failures contain? — `AllocFailure<T>` returns rejected `T`; operation-specific recovery.
201:- **Q1.10** Are capacity and addresses observable? — Observable capacity; explicit raw-address exposure.
202:- **Q1.11** What does “unchanged” require of allocator state? — Availability predicate plus four laws: progress, unchanged state on failure, release never reduces availability, a concrete success witness.
203:- **Q1.12** What rolls back around callbacks? — No rollback of callback effects; every element accounted for in a typed result.
204:- **Q1.13** Can resetting the system allocator return `Allocator<Sys>` again? — Fresh brand after any supported system reset.
205:- **Q2.1** Are storage release, destruction and resource cleanup separate? — Separate storage release, destruction and resource cleanup.
206:- **Q2.2** Which containers implement `Destroy`? — Exactly sixteen named Destroy impls: Array (T: Destroy); Heap, HeapArray and Vec (two each: T: Copy; T: Destroy); String (unconditional); Option (T: Destroy); Result (three: T: Destroy/E: Copy, T: Destroy/E: Destroy, T: Copy/E: Destroy); OrderedMap (four: each of K and V Copy or Destroy). HashMap excluded. <a id="q2-2-revision"></a>Revised 2026-11-06 by [information flow](#decided-project-infoflow-2026-11-06): `Secret<T>` is `Copy` only for scalar `T`; secret arrays and heap secrets are linear and their destruction wipes them.
207:- **Q2.3** In what order are contents destroyed? — Index order 0..len−1, then the backing; OrderedMap in ascending key order, key then value, then the nodes.
208:- **Q2.4** How does a container of non-`Destroy` resources release its storage? — `drain(v, ctx, f)` with `f: fn(&mut C, T) with(K) -> Step<T, E>`; the only way to empty a container of non-Destroy elements.
209:- **Q2.5** What happens when drain processing fails? — On Stop, `Drained { at, survivor, rest, err }`; `map_into` returns `Stopped { done, survivor, rest, err }`.
210:- **Q2.6** Must `destroy` terminate and avoid traps? — Ordinary partial correctness; prove stronger guarantees separately.
211:- **Q2.7** Does user destruction have special execution semantics? — Ordinary consuming checked `destroy`; at most `Alloc`.
212:- **Q2.8** How is 'Copy or Destroy' written for container impls? — (a) separate impls for every admitted combination of Copy/Destroy payload bounds, no disjunctive-bound syntax: two each for Heap, HeapArray and Vec, three for Result, four for OrderedMap; coherence may use Copy/Destroy disjointness. *Revises or adds: TRAIT-1@1 (under OD6): equal generic impl heads allowed only when Copy/Destroy disjointness makes their domains disjoint; all other coherence and orphan rules unchanged. Option (b) would also add new bound syntax.*
213:- **Q3.1** What does opening a heap array produce? — Second-class shared/mutable array views; no element references.
214:- **Q3.2** How are views passed and lent? — Downward parameters; mutable lending freezes origin.
215:- **Q3.3** What is a view’s root identity? — Original owner identity plus projection path.
216:- **Q3.4** Which view accesses conflict? — Existing whole-root conflict rules.
217:- **Q3.5** What is the index type? — `u64` lengths/indices across profiles; checked target limits.
218:- **Q3.6** How are nested linear elements traversed without substitutes? — `drain` / `map_into` for consuming traversal; `swap_at(v, i, x) -> T` for in-place replacement.
219:- **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises.
220:- **Q3.8** How do zero-sized elements behave? — Zero-sized slots own their obligations; they occupy no bytes and may share addresses.
221:- **Q3.9** What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean? — `expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance.
222:- **Q3.10** What happens when address space runs out? — A collision means overlapping occupied bytes; address exhaustion is part of Avail.
223:- **Q3.11** How are typed values related to raw bytes? — Structured values and raw store joined by `Rep`.
224:- **Q3.12** How are extern pointer parameters marked, and how does C keep memory past the call? — (a) `kept` parameter marker; `Kept<H>` receipt ended only by the paired extern. *Revises or adds: New design + spelling: owner decision.*
225:- **Q4.1** What is a literal’s type? — Copy `StaticStr`; explicit fallible dynamic conversion.
226:- **Q4.2** Which encoding and NUL policy apply? — UTF-8 including embedded NUL.
227:- **Q4.3** What do length and comparison mean? — Byte length; content equality; lexicographic byte order.
228:- **Q4.4** Are literal addresses observable and literals mergeable? — Content semantics; pooling allowed; no safe address identity.
229:- **Q4.5** What is the FFI representation? — Explicit pointer/byte-length FFI adapter.
230:- **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.*
231:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
232:- **Q5.1** Should capability arguments ever be inferred? — Explicit capability arguments on every polymorphic call. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
233:- **Q5.2** May explicit arguments contain the caller’s capability variables? — Explicit symbolic capability expressions and union.
234:- **Q5.3** What is capability-set equality and normal form? — Canonical semantic equality; refuse repeated names.
235:- **Q5.4** Is a capability inference solver still normative? — Explicit substitution checking; no inference solver. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Bounded runs: a timeout is INCOMPLETE, never a failure and never a pass.
#
# The rule (docs/project/PROMOTION_PIPELINE.md, result vocabulary): a test that exceeds its time budget
# has produced no observation. Under machine load a freshly linked binary or a compile can overrun
# a budget sized for an idle machine (test_ssa.sh once reported hundreds of 5-second alarms as
# failures), so a timeout is re-run once, alone, with a larger budget:
#   - the re-run completes      -> its result is judged normally (pass or FAIL), recorded "recovered";
#   - the re-run times out too  -> INCOMPLETE: not a pass, not a failure. A gate with an incomplete
#                                  result exits nonzero, and a qualifying run contains none.
#
# Each attempt runs under lib/bounded_exec.py, which makes the command the leader of a new process
# group, reports its outcome through a status file (never through the command's own streams, so
# nothing the command prints can fake or hide a timeout) and, when the budget expires, sends SIGTERM
# and then SIGKILL to the whole group, so no descendant outlives the attempt into the retry.
#
# Sourcing (`BOUNDED_PARENT="$TMP"; source scripts/checks/lib/bounded.sh`) gives the gate PRIVATE
# state: a fresh directory (mktemp under BOUNDED_PARENT, else $TMPDIR) with an outcome file and a
# log. Every sourcing creates its own directory, so nested or parallel gates never share state.
# With BOUNDED_PARENT under the gate's own scratch directory, its cleanup removes the state too.
#
#   bounded_run [-i STDIN_FILE] [-r RETRY_SECS] SECS -- CMD [ARGS...]
#     Runs CMD (an executable, not a shell function). Its stdout and stderr are buffered per
#     attempt and only a COMPLETED attempt's output is written: an incomplete run writes nothing,
#     so output-matching checks never pass on the partial output of a run that hung. Without -i the
#     command inherits stdin, which a re-run cannot replay: callers that feed input pass -i.
#     Returns CMD's status, or 124 when it timed out on both attempts or the runner reported no
#     outcome. SECS and RETRY_SECS are positive decimal seconds. A command may itself exit
#     124, so test with `bounded_is_incomplete "$rc"`, which also reads the outcome bounded_run
#     recorded (this works across `( cd dir && bounded_run ... )` subshells; run one bounded_run
#     at a time per sourcing). RETRY_SECS defaults to BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR (2) times SECS.
#   bounded_summary
#     Lists this gate's recovered and incomplete runs; gates print it before their final tally.
#   bounded_watchdog SECS  -> sets the array TO for the watchdog form: "${TO[@]}" CMD [ARGS...]
#     runs CMD through `bash bounded.sh SECS` in a separate process with the same retry; on a second
#     timeout it writes no command output, prints "INCOMPLETE: ..." on stderr and exits 124. Its
#     recovered and incomplete lines go to this gate's log (handed over on its command line).
#   bounded_gate_status
#     The end-of-gate verdict for a gate using the watchdog form: lists recovered, incomplete and
#     runner-failed runs, removes the state directory, and returns 1 when any run was incomplete or
#     the runner failed, so the gate exits nonzero whatever its own checks concluded from a hung
#     command's empty output. Last line:
#       bounded_gate_status && [ "$FAIL" -eq 0 ]
#
# If the runner itself fails (no outcome reported), the command's output is discarded and the run
# is incomplete (status 124, a "runner-failed" log line): never a pass. If the runner is cancelled (the
# gate is interrupted or terminated), it kills the command's group first.
#
# Without python3 commands run directly with no watchdog: one warning when the gate sources this
# file, never inside a command's output, and the watchdog form then runs commands directly too.
# Such a run is never incomplete.

BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR="${BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR:-2}"
if [[ "$BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR" =~ ^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$ ]] && awk -v f="$BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR" 'BEGIN { exit !(f + 0 >= 1) }'; then :
else echo "bounded.sh: BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR must be a number >= 1; using 2" >&2; BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR=2; fi
# _bounded_secs_ok S: S is a positive decimal number of seconds (fractions allowed).
_bounded_secs_ok() { [[ "$1" =~ ^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?$ ]] && awk -v s="$1" 'BEGIN { exit !(s + 0 > 0) }'; }
# Fixed-point, trailing zeros trimmed: never scientific notation, which _bounded_secs_ok rejects.
_bounded_mul() { awk -v s="$1" -v f="$2" 'BEGIN { r = sprintf("%.6f", s * f); sub(/0+$/, "", r); sub(/\.$/, "", r); print r }'; }
BOUNDED_SH="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/$(basename "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")"
BOUNDED_EXEC="$(dirname "$BOUNDED_SH")/bounded_exec.py"
BOUNDED_DIR="$(mktemp -d "${BOUNDED_PARENT:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/bounded.XXXXXX")"
BOUNDED_OUTCOME_FILE="$BOUNDED_DIR/outcome"
BOUNDED_LOG="$BOUNDED_DIR/log"
# The watchdog form appends to its caller's log, handed over explicitly on its command line.
if [ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" = "$0" ]; then
  # A standalone watchdog removes its state however it ends.
  trap 'rm -rf "$BOUNDED_DIR"' EXIT
  [ -z "${BOUNDED_WATCHDOG_LOG:-}" ] || BOUNDED_LOG="$BOUNDED_WATCHDOG_LOG"
fi
: >> "$BOUNDED_LOG"
BOUNDED_HAVE_WATCHDOG=1
if ! command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  BOUNDED_HAVE_WATCHDOG=0
  [ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" = "$0" ] || echo "  warn python3 not found — commands run without a hang watchdog" >&2
fi

bounded_is_incomplete() {
  [ "$1" = 124 ] && [ "$(cat "$BOUNDED_OUTCOME_FILE" 2>/dev/null)" = incomplete ]
}

bounded_run() {
  local stdin_file="" retry="" secs="" rc tries=0 budget tmp outcome
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      -i) stdin_file="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      -r) retry="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --) shift; break ;;
      *) if [ -z "$secs" ]; then secs="$1"; shift; else break; fi ;;
    esac
  done
  [ -n "$secs" ] && [ $# -gt 0 ] || { echo "bounded_run: usage: bounded_run [-i FILE] [-r SECS] SECS -- CMD..." >&2; return 2; }
  [ -n "$retry" ] || retry="$(_bounded_mul "$secs" "$BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR")"
  if ! _bounded_secs_ok "$secs" || ! _bounded_secs_ok "$retry"; then
    echo "bounded_run: budgets must be positive numbers of seconds (got $secs, retry $retry)" >&2; return 2
  fi
  echo done > "$BOUNDED_OUTCOME_FILE"
  if [ "$BOUNDED_HAVE_WATCHDOG" != 1 ]; then
    if [ -n "$stdin_file" ]; then "$@" < "$stdin_file"; else "$@"; fi
    return $?
  fi
  tmp="$(mktemp -d "$BOUNDED_DIR/run.XXXXXX")"
  budget="$secs"
  while :; do
    tries=$((tries + 1))
    rm -f "$tmp/status"
    if [ -n "$stdin_file" ]; then
      python3 -I "$BOUNDED_EXEC" --timeout "$budget" --kill-after "${BOUNDED_KILL_AFTER:-10}" --status "$tmp/status" -- "$@" < "$stdin_file" > "$tmp/out" 2> "$tmp/err"
    else
      python3 -I "$BOUNDED_EXEC" --timeout "$budget" --kill-after "${BOUNDED_KILL_AFTER:-10}" --status "$tmp/status" -- "$@" > "$tmp/out" 2> "$tmp/err"
    fi
    outcome="$(cat "$tmp/status" 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$outcome" in
      "exit "*) break ;;
      timeout) [ "$tries" = 1 ] || break; budget="$retry" ;;
      *) outcome=runner-failed; break ;;
    esac
  done
  if [ "$outcome" = timeout ]; then
    echo incomplete > "$BOUNDED_OUTCOME_FILE"
    echo "incomplete ${secs}s+${retry}s $*" >> "$BOUNDED_LOG"
    rc=124
  elif [ "$outcome" = runner-failed ]; then
    # The runner reported nothing (it failed or was cancelled): no observation, so the run is
    # incomplete exactly as a double timeout is (status 124, bounded_is_incomplete true).
    echo "bounded_run: the runner reported no outcome for: $*" >&2
    echo incomplete > "$BOUNDED_OUTCOME_FILE"
    echo "runner-failed ${secs}s $*" >> "$BOUNDED_LOG"
    rc=124
  else
    cat "$tmp/out"; cat "$tmp/err" >&2
    rc="${outcome#exit }"
    [ "$tries" = 2 ] && echo "recovered ${secs}s $*" >> "$BOUNDED_LOG"
  fi
  rm -rf "$tmp"
  return "$rc"
}

bounded_summary() {
  local nrec ninc
  nrec=$(grep -c '^recovered' "$BOUNDED_LOG" 2>/dev/null || true); nrec=${nrec:-0}
  ninc=$(grep -c '^incomplete\|^runner-failed' "$BOUNDED_LOG" 2>/dev/null || true); ninc=${ninc:-0}
  [ "$nrec" -gt 0 ] || [ "$ninc" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  echo "Bounded runs: $nrec recovered (timed out, then completed alone), $ninc incomplete (timed out twice, or the runner failed):"
  sed 's/^/  /' "$BOUNDED_LOG"
}

bounded_watchdog() {
  if [ "$BOUNDED_HAVE_WATCHDOG" = 1 ]; then
    TO=(env "BOUNDED_WATCHDOG_LOG=$BOUNDED_LOG" "BOUNDED_PARENT=$BOUNDED_DIR" bash "$BOUNDED_SH" "$1")
  else
    TO=(env)  # no python3: run commands directly (the gate warned once when it sourced this file)
  fi
}

bounded_gate_status() {
  local ninc
  bounded_summary
  ninc=$(grep -c '^incomplete\|^runner-failed' "$BOUNDED_LOG" 2>/dev/null || true); ninc=${ninc:-0}
  [ "$ninc" -eq 0 ] || echo "INCOMPLETE: $ninc command(s) timed out twice or lost their runner: this run is incomplete, not passed"
  rm -rf "$BOUNDED_DIR"
  [ "$ninc" -eq 0 ]
}

# Watchdog form: `bash bounded.sh SECS CMD...` (through bounded_watchdog's TO array).
if [ "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" = "$0" ]; then
  [ $# -ge 2 ] || { echo "usage: bash $0 SECS CMD [ARGS...]" >&2; exit 2; }
  secs="$1"; shift
  bounded_run "$secs" -- "$@"; rc=$?
  if bounded_is_incomplete "$rc"; then
    echo "INCOMPLETE: timed out twice (${secs}s, then $(_bounded_mul "$secs" "$BOUNDED_RETRY_FACTOR")s alone) or lost its runner: $*" >&2
  fi
  exit "$rc"
fi

exec
/bin/bash -lc "cat scripts/proofs/lean_lex.py; cat scripts/spec/rules.lean; sed -n '200,290p' _inputs/FOUNDATION.md; nl -ba scripts/spec/rules.py; rg -n 'O1|PathOk|normalized' ConcreteSpec/README.md; rg -n 'Q[0-9]+\\.[0-9]+|OD[0-9]+' docs/decisions.md | tail -8" in /tmp/claude-30035/-home-unbalancedparen-projects-concrete2/e2c54e65-31b5-4145-8101-c2d7b071b97e/scratchpad/rev-sc2/codex
 succeeded in 0ms:
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A lexer for the Lean sources of ConcreteSpec, shared by the proof-integrity checks.

`lex(src)` returns `src` with every comment (nested `/- -/`, `--`) blanked to spaces, newlines kept,
so offsets and line numbers are the source's. Everything else is kept, string literals included.
It follows Lean's literal forms, so no comment delimiter inside a literal starts a comment: a
string with its escapes, raw strings r"…" and r#"…"#, character literals and quoted names «…».
Whether a string interpolates depends on the parser, not the lexer, so interpolation is admitted
only directly after `s!`, `m!` or `f!` (its `{ }` code, with its own strings and comments, is
lexed recursively), and any other string holding `{` is an error. An unterminated string,
comment, interpolation or name is an error too, and so is a syntax quotation `(…) (whose comments are
syntax data; the library runs no meta code): the lexer fails closed, never skips.
`comment_spans(src)` gives the top-level comments (the owner view, scripts/spec/lean_site.py, takes
its doc blocks from them, so a doc comment inside an ordinary comment or a string is not one).

Commands (exit 3 when a source cannot be read or lexed):

  scan RE FILE...          `file:line: text` for every match of RE (over the whole lexed text,
                           reported at the line where it starts) and for every `import` outside
                           the header, which must be lines `import ConcreteSpec[.M…]`, one
                           module each, ended by a command at column 0
  examples SRC DST FILE... copies each FILE (a path relative to SRC) to DST with every `example`
                           command turned into `noncomputable def «⟪example⟫FILE:LINE»` (no space,
                           so listings split on spaces stay sound), so the kernel's view of it
                           stays in the environment; prints the count. Every
                           `example` token must start a line.
"""
import os, re, sys

EXAMPLE_MARK = "⟪example⟫"


class LexError(Exception):
    pass


def lex(src, spans=None, literals=True):
    """`src` with its comments blanked; when `spans` is a list, each top-level comment's
    (start, end) offsets are appended to it (a comment nested in another is part of that one).
    With `literals=False`, string, character and name literals are blanked too (code tokens only)."""
    n, out = len(src), []

    def emit(text, literal=False):
        if literal and not literals:
            text = "".join("\n" if ch == "\n" else " " for ch in text)
        out.append(text)

    def ident(i):                                     # src[i] continues an identifier
        return i >= 0 and (src[i].isalnum() or src[i] in "_'!?." or ord(src[i]) > 127)

    def comment(i):                                   # at "/-": blank to the matching "-/"
        start, depth = i, 0
        while i < n:
            if src.startswith("/-", i): depth += 1; out.append("  "); i += 2
            elif src.startswith("-/", i):
                depth -= 1; out.append("  "); i += 2
                if depth == 0:
                    if spans is not None: spans.append((start, i))
                    return i
            else: out.append("\n" if src[i] == "\n" else " "); i += 1
        raise LexError("unterminated comment")

    def string(i):                                    # at '"'
        interp = src[i - 2:i] in ("s!", "m!", "f!") and not ident(i - 3)
        emit('"', True); i += 1
        while i < n:
            c = src[i]
            if c == "\\": emit(src[i:i + 2], True); i += 2
            elif c == '"': emit(c, True); return i + 1
            elif c == "{" and interp: emit(c, True); i = code(i + 1, True)
            elif c == "{":
                line = src.count("\n", 0, i) + 1
                raise LexError(f"line {line}: a string holding '{{' without s!, m! or f! before it")
            else: emit(c, True); i += 1
        raise LexError("unterminated string")

    def raw(i):                                       # at 'r' of r#*"
        j = i + 1
        while j < n and src[j] == "#": j += 1
        close = '"' + "#" * (j - i - 1)
        k = src.find(close, j + 1)
        if k < 0: raise LexError("unterminated raw string")
        emit(src[i:k + len(close)], True); return k + len(close)

    def code(i, inner):                               # inner: inside an interpolation, stop at its '}'
        depth = 0
        while i < n:
            c = src[i]
            if src.startswith("/-", i): i = comment(i)
            elif src.startswith("--", i):
                j = src.find("\n", i); j = n if j < 0 else j; out.append(" " * (j - i)); i = j
            elif c == '"': i = string(i)
            elif c == "«":                            # a quoted name part: no comment inside it
                k = src.find("»", i + 1)
                if k < 0: raise LexError("unterminated «name»")
                emit(src[i:k + 1], True); i = k + 1
            elif src.startswith("`(", i) or src.startswith("``(", i):
                # A syntax quotation: its comments are syntax data, not comments of the source,
                # and the library runs no meta code, so the lexer refuses it.
                raise LexError(f"line {src.count(chr(10), 0, i) + 1}: a syntax quotation")
            elif c == "r" and not ident(i - 1) and re.match(r'r#*"', src[i:]): i = raw(i)
            elif c == "'" and not ident(i - 1):
                m = re.match(r"'(\\(x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|.)|[^\\'\n])'", src[i:])
                k = len(m.group(0)) if m else 1
                emit(src[i:i + k], m is not None); i += k
            elif inner and c == "{": depth += 1; out.append(c); i += 1
            elif inner and c == "}":
                out.append(c); i += 1
                if depth == 0: return i
                depth -= 1
            else: out.append(c); i += 1
        if inner: raise LexError("unterminated interpolation")
        return i

    code(0, False)
    return "".join(out)


def comment_spans(src):
    """The (start, end) offsets of every top-level comment of `src`, in order."""
    spans = []
    lex(src, spans)
    return spans


HEADER = re.compile(r"import ConcreteSpec(\.[A-Za-z0-9_]+)*\s*$")
FIRST_COMMAND = re.compile(r"(namespace|open|section|def|theorem|structure|inductive|abbrev)\b")


def scan(pattern, paths):
    word, rc = re.compile(pattern, re.M), 0
    for path in paths:
        try:
            text = lex(open(path, encoding="utf-8").read())
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, LexError) as e:
            print(f"error: {path}: {e}", file=sys.stderr); rc = 3; continue
        lines = text.split("\n")
        # Lean lets an import list continue (`import A B`, or B on the next line), so the first
        # line after the header must be a command at column 0.
        k = 0
        while k < len(lines) and (not lines[k].strip() or HEADER.match(lines[k])): k += 1
        if k < len(lines) and not FIRST_COMMAND.match(lines[k]):
            print(f"{path}:{k + 1}: {lines[k].strip()} (the import header must end in a command at column 0)")
        hits = {text.count("\n", 0, m.start()) + 1 for m in word.finditer(text)}
        hits |= {j for j, line in enumerate(lines, 1)
                 if re.search(r"\bimport\b", line) and not (j <= k and HEADER.match(line))}
        for j in sorted(hits):
            print(f"{path}:{j}: {lines[j - 1].strip()}")
    return rc


# `example` as a command token: not part of a longer identifier (`example'`, `my.example`, `examples`)
EXAMPLE = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.'!?])example(?![\w'!?])")


def count_examples(src):
    """The number of `example` commands in a source (code tokens only, not literal contents)."""
    return len(EXAMPLE.findall(lex(src, literals=False)))


def examples(src_dir, dst_dir, paths):
    count = 0
    for rel in paths:
        src = open(os.path.join(src_dir, rel), encoding="utf-8").read()
        text = lex(src, literals=False)          # an `example` inside a literal is not a command
        out, last = [], 0
        for m in EXAMPLE.finditer(text):
            line = text.count("\n", 0, m.start()) + 1
            if m.start() > 0 and text[m.start() - 1] != "\n":
                raise LexError(f"{rel}:{line}: an `example` that does not start its line")
            out.append(src[last:m.start()])
            out.append(f"noncomputable def «{EXAMPLE_MARK}{rel}:{line}»")
            last = m.end(); count += 1
        out.append(src[last:])
        dst = os.path.join(dst_dir, rel)
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dst) or ".", exist_ok=True)
        open(dst, "w", encoding="utf-8").write("".join(out))
    print(count)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        if sys.argv[1] == "scan":
            sys.exit(scan(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3:]))
        if sys.argv[1] == "examples":
            examples(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4:]); sys.exit(0)
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, LexError) as e:
        print(f"error: {e}", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(3)
    print(f"usage: {sys.argv[0]} scan RE FILE... | examples SRC DST FILE...", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)
-- Emits one JSON object per registered rule (ConcreteSpec/Spec/Rules.lean), for scripts/spec/rules.py.
-- Run from the repository root: `lake env lean scripts/spec/rules.lean > rules.jsonl`.
import ConcreteSpec
import Lean
open Lean Elab Command

#eval show CommandElabM Unit from do
  let env ← getEnv
  for r in ConcreteSpec.Core.registeredRules do
    let doc := (← findDocString? env r.name).getD ""
    IO.println (Json.compress (Json.mkObj [("name", toString r.name), ("rev", r.rev),
      ("exists", toJson (env.contains r.name)), ("doc", doc)]))
  IO.println (Json.compress (Json.mkObj [("end", toJson true)]))
     1	#!/usr/bin/env python3
     2	"""The rule gate's checks (scripts/spec/rules.sh): the registered rules against the sources.
     3	
     4	  rules.py RULES.jsonl SRC_DIR ROWS...
     5	      RULES.jsonl is scripts/spec/rules.lean's output; SRC_DIR holds the specification's .lean
     6	      sources (searched recursively, at least one); each ROWS file is a decision inventory
     7	      (docs/decisions.md, ROADMAP.md) in which a cited decision row must appear.
     8	
     9	Prints `ok`/`FAIL` lines; exits 1 on any failure, 3 when an input cannot be read or lexed.
    10	A rule's doc comment must cite a decision row that exists in an inventory; each rule needs one
    11	example whose doc comment says ``Accepted (rule `Name`)`` and a different one whose doc comment
    12	says ``Refused (rule `Name`)``; a verdict doc comment holds no nested comment and no rule's two
    13	verdicts at once; every ``(rule `Name`)`` anywhere in a source (either kind of comment, or a
    14	literal) must name a registered rule; no rule is registered twice; revisions are >= 1.
    15	"""
    16	import json, os, re, sys
    17	
    18	sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "..", "proofs"))
    19	import lean_lex  # noqa: E402
    20	
    21	ROW = re.compile(r"\b(Q\d+(?:\.\d+)+|OD\d+|P\d+[a-z]?|R-\d{4})\b")
    22	# A rule name is a Lean name as `toString` prints it: dotted components, a component that needs
    23	# quoting written «…» (which may hold spaces). Anything up to the closing backtick is the name.
    24	CITE = re.compile(r"\(rule `([^`\n]+)`\)")
    25	VERDICT = re.compile(r"\b(Accepted|Refused) \(rule `([^`\n]+)`\)")
    26	# What may stand between a doc comment and the `example` it documents: attributes and modifiers.
    27	EXAMPLE_AFTER = re.compile(
    28	    r"\s*(?:@\[(?:[^\[\]]|\[[^\[\]]*\])*\]\s*)*(?:(?:noncomputable|private|protected|unsafe|partial)\s+)*"
    29	    r"example(?![\w'!?])")
    30	
    31	
    32	def row_exists(row, inventories):
    33	    pat = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.-])" + re.escape(row) + r"(?![\w-]|\.\d)")
    34	    return any(pat.search(t) for t in inventories)
    35	
    36	
    37	def main(rules_path, src_dir, row_paths):
    38	    fails = []
    39	    try:
    40	        rules = [json.loads(l) for l in open(rules_path, encoding="utf-8") if l.strip()]
    41	        inventories = [open(p, encoding="utf-8").read() for p in row_paths]
    42	    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError) as e:
    43	        print(f"error: {e}", file=sys.stderr); return 3
    44	    if not rules or rules[-1] != {"end": True}:
    45	        print("error: the rule listing is truncated (no end record)", file=sys.stderr); return 3
    46	    if not inventories:
    47	        print("error: no decision inventory given", file=sys.stderr); return 3
    48	    if not os.path.isdir(src_dir):
    49	        print(f"error: {src_dir}: not a directory", file=sys.stderr); return 3
    50	    rules = rules[:-1]
    51	    names = [r["name"] for r in rules]
    52	    for n in sorted({n for n in names if names.count(n) > 1}):
    53	        fails.append(f"rule {n} is registered more than once")
    54	    for r in rules:
    55	        if not r["exists"]: fails.append(f"rule {r['name']} names no declaration")
    56	        if r["rev"] < 1: fails.append(f"rule {r['name']} has revision {r['rev']} (revisions start at 1)")
    57	        if not r["doc"].strip(): fails.append(f"rule {r['name']} has no doc comment")
    58	        else:
    59	            rows = [m.group(1) for m in ROW.finditer(r["doc"])]
    60	            if not rows: fails.append(f"rule {r['name']}'s doc comment cites no decision row")
    61	            elif not any(row_exists(x, inventories) for x in rows):
    62	                fails.append(f"rule {r['name']}'s doc comment cites no existing decision row ({', '.join(rows)})")
    63	    seen = {n: set() for n in names}
    64	    scanned = 0
    65	
    66	    def walk_error(e):
    67	        raise e
    68	    try:
    69	        tree = sorted(os.walk(src_dir, onerror=walk_error))
    70	    except OSError as e:
    71	        print(f"error: {src_dir}: {e}", file=sys.stderr); return 3
    72	    for dirpath, _, files in tree:
    73	        for f in sorted(files):
    74	            if not f.endswith(".lean"): continue
    75	            path = os.path.join(dirpath, f)
    76	            try:
    77	                src = open(path, encoding="utf-8").read(); spans = []
    78	                lean_lex.lex(src, spans)
    79	            except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, lean_lex.LexError) as e:
    80	                print(f"error: {path}: {e}", file=sys.stderr); return 3
    81	            scanned += 1
    82	            # citations anywhere in the file (comments of both kinds, and literals: fail closed)
    83	            for m in CITE.finditer(src):
    84	                if m.group(1) not in seen:
    85	                    line = src.count("\n", 0, m.start()) + 1
    86	                    fails.append(f"{path}:{line}: cites unregistered rule {m.group(1)}")
    87	            # a verdict anywhere outside a doc comment (code, a string literal) is stray too
    88	            for m in VERDICT.finditer(src):
    89	                if not any(a <= m.start() < b and src.startswith("/--", a) for a, b in spans):
    90	                    line = src.count("\n", 0, m.start()) + 1
    91	                    fails.append(f"{path}:{line}: a verdict citation outside an example's doc comment")
    92	            for a, b in spans:
    93	                text, line = src[a:b], src.count("\n", 0, a) + 1
    94	                verdicts = VERDICT.findall(text)
    95	                if not verdicts: continue
    96	                if not (text.startswith("/--") and EXAMPLE_AFTER.match(src, b)):
    97	                    fails.append(f"{path}:{line}: a verdict citation outside an example's doc comment")
    98	                    continue
    99	                if "/-" in text[3:]:
   100	                    fails.append(f"{path}:{line}: a verdict doc comment holds a nested comment")
   101	                    continue
   102	                kinds = {}
   103	                for v, n in verdicts: kinds.setdefault(n, set()).add(v)
   104	                both = sorted(n for n, k in kinds.items() if len(k) > 1)
   105	                if both:
   106	                    fails.append(f"{path}:{line}: one example is both accepted and refused by {', '.join(both)}")
   107	                    continue
   108	                for n, k in kinds.items():
   109	                    if n in seen: seen[n] |= k
   110	    if scanned == 0:
   111	        print(f"error: {src_dir}: no .lean source", file=sys.stderr); return 3
   112	    for n in names:
   113	        for v in ("Accepted", "Refused"):
   114	            if v not in seen[n]: fails.append(f"rule {n} has no example marked {v} (rule `{n}`)")
   115	    for f in fails: print(f"  FAIL {f}")
   116	    if not fails: print(f"  ok   {len(rules)} registered rules in {scanned} sources, each documented with an"
   117	                        " existing decision row and an accepted and a refused example; every citation"
   118	                        " names a registered rule")
   119	    return 1 if fails else 0
   120	
   121	
   122	if __name__ == "__main__":
   123	    if len(sys.argv) < 4:
   124	        print(f"usage: {sys.argv[0]} RULES.jsonl SRC_DIR ROWS...", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(2)
   125	    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3:]))
