173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
182:- **The generated-chapter requirement** (rows Q9.15, P3): Lean is the specification, in one main normative file with doc comments and examples; no prose is generated from it. This confirms the [2026-11-04 decision](#decided-project-lean-is-the-spec). The "one main normative file" wording is revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): one authoritative specification library in cohesive modules, with `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` as its public import.
188:<a id="decided-2026-11-05-new-rules"></a>**New rules** (P4): duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl are refused, like duplicate fields (bug 313); the rule gets its ID and diagnostic when it is written in Lean.
283:- **P1** Where agent discretion ends — Claude and Codex reason every question from the philosophy and recommend; they never pick a language answer through code, fixtures, diagnostics or docs. 'Important' = anything that revises a decided rule, changes observable language meaning you have not ruled on, or that you reserved. Every important decision comes to you.
284:- **P2** Priority order when principles conflict — Soundness and provability > transparency (with auditability, honest trust boundaries and compiler-owned identity) > one authoritative definition (checked derived views allowed) > explicitness at the use site > brevity. The philosophy guides open choices and never silently reopens decided rules.
285:- **P3** Lean is the spec: confirm the amendment — Lean is the specification: confirmed together with Q9.15 (revision below).
286:- **P4** Duplicate enum variants or methods (bug 313) — Refuse duplicate variant names in one enum and duplicate method names in one impl, with a new rule and diagnostic, the same as duplicate fields.
287:- **P5** legacy/ is a byte-frozen snapshot, never patched or run — legacy/ is main's tree at the move commit, byte-frozen. A root check compares its tree hash, allowing only deletions a satisfied RETENTION.tsv entry authorizes. No legacy gate ever runs. The oracle is the tag-built artifact (P13), not the legacy/ copy.
288:- **P6** Retention manifest gates deleting legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv at the root gives every legacy asset (corpus, regressions, examples, std, bug ledger, site, docs, research) a destination and deletion criterion. Revised 2026-11-06 ([restart row](#decided-project-restart)): nothing is deleted at B3; legacy/, the old pipeline included, is deleted way later (LD: once the new compiler implements a full real program such as RocksDB, ethrex or similar), when RETENTION.tsv has no live entry, a final ideas sweep has dispositioned every idea and the owner explicitly approves.
289:- **P7** Prove the new landing path before moving — T0: land beside the old tree the new root CI, required-checks.txt (enforced by train watch and land), RETENTION.tsv, the task disposition table, ORACLE.lock with the oracle artifact, and ISSUES.tsv. T1: rehearse the move on a throwaway branch: fresh clone, build, hook, exact-SHA CI, train --dry-run land; a broken build, a failing gate and a missing required check must each be refused. T2: the move in one train.
290:- **P8** Server services under scripts/ — Keep only landing services: train, review, hook, landing bot. The status page, work queue, fuzz farm, perf tracking and nightly campaign all stop and are recorded as archived in RETENTION.tsv; each returns only with a roadmap task.
291:- **P9** Website during the transition — The website goes down at the move and is redone from scratch with the new compiler; no frozen playground is served. Unpublishing the live site is an outward action whose moment the owner confirms.
292:- **P10** What 'closed' means for old-compiler bugs — Old-compiler defects become 'retired with the old implementation', never 'fixed'. Each keeps its reproducer, ruling and structural class; 106 reproducers feed the oracle comparisons. Bugs the frozen browser compiler still shows stay visible while the site serves it.
293:- **P11** Std bugs and rulings are carried — Add a 'std' class: 12 std bugs move with std as contracts plus regressions. The 112 language rulings (26 still open) keep their ruling and become conformance examples.
294:- **P12** Issue record from day one — From day one, ISSUES.tsv at the root: id, date, area (spec|proof|compiler|launcher|ci|migration), evidence, owner, severity, closure condition, status, with a small root check. Spec, proof, launcher, CI and migration defects go there. The full bug ledger and tool return with slice 1.
295:- **P13** concrete-ci-2 as the only CI host — CI runs on concrete-ci-2 (self-hosted) and on GitHub-hosted runners as a regular second pool, not only a fallback. Jobs that need ci-2 caches run there; the oracle is fetched as a release asset verified by digest; a check neither pool can run is incomplete and blocks landing. Self-hosted runners never run pull_request jobs.
296:- **P14** ci-3 handover: an exception to the archive requirement — Done by the owner on 2026-11-04: concrete-ci-3 was handed to stabileo; its fuzz corpus, perf history and leanprof data were not copied (an exception to the archive requirement).
297:- **P15** New roadmap: retiring obligations — Accept the new single-file ROADMAP.md (wip/new-roadmap, reconciled: prepare → rehearse → move, assurance wording per Q8.1, Phase E per P18, decides nothing itself): 79 tasks kept as rows, 345 folded into area tasks with their obligations, 109 deferred with triggers, 7 retired, 15 done.
298:- **P16** Status changes in the refusal list — Accept the new statuses in PHILOSOPHY.md. Now Permanent: operator overloading (OD26), capability hiding (authority rule 5), derived equality, mutable global state (OD8), umbrella capabilities and aliases, old/quantifiers in contracts (OD117), lexical shadowing (OD67), and ghost code that affects execution. Ghost code itself is admitted as decided (OD81). Foreign function values reopen when typed extern fn types are admitted (OD4); concurrency reopens on R-0399's trigger.
299:- **P17a** Default arguments — Default arguments: a Permanent refusal, reopened only by a new decision from you. They conflict with visible arguments.
    63	  dataSpace : AddrSpace
    64	  null : Nat
    65	  maxObject : Nat
    66	  deriving DecidableEq, Repr
    67	
    68	/-- What every profile must satisfy.
    69	
    70	- An address has at least one bit, and a stored pointer has room for all of them.
    71	- A stored pointer's alignment is a power of two, and its size is a multiple of it (the layout law
    72	  that a size is a multiple of its alignment, FOUNDATION C3).
    73	- A stored pointer fits in one object.
    74	- A stored length has between 1 and 64 bits, since its meaning is a `u64` (Q3.5).
    75	- The data space is one of the target's spaces.
    76	- The null address is an address.
    77	- An object, and the address one past its end, fit in the address range: `maxObject` is below
    78	  `2 ^ addrBits`, the analogue of row Q3.10's one-past bound `base + size ≤ 2^64 − 1` that every
    79	  profile needs (question 3). -/
    80	def Target.WellFormed (t : Target) : Prop :=
    81	  0 < t.addrBits ∧ t.addrBits ≤ 8 * t.ptrSize ∧ IsPow2 t.ptrAlign ∧ t.ptrSize % t.ptrAlign = 0 ∧
    82	  t.ptrSize ≤ t.maxObject ∧
    83	  0 < t.lenBits ∧ t.lenBits ≤ 64 ∧ t.dataSpace ∈ t.spaces ∧
    84	  t.null < 2 ^ t.addrBits ∧ t.maxObject < 2 ^ t.addrBits
    85	
    86	instance (t : Target) : Decidable t.WellFormed := by unfold Target.WellFormed; infer_instance
    87	
    88	/-- The existing target, linux-x86_64-hosted (row Q7.1): 64-bit addresses, 8-byte pointers aligned
    89	to 8, 64-bit lengths, little-endian, one data space, null at address 0, and objects of at most
    90	`2^64 − 1` bytes (row Q3.10). -/
    91	def linuxX8664 : Target where
    92	  addrBits := 64
    93	  ptrSize := 8
    94	  ptrAlign := 8
    95	  lenBits := 64
    96	  byteOrder := .little
    97	  spaces := [⟨0⟩]
    98	  dataSpace := ⟨0⟩
    99	  null := 0
   100	  maxObject := 2 ^ 64 - 1
   101	
   102	/-! ### Examples -/
   103	
   104	/-- The existing profile is well formed. -/
   105	example : linuxX8664.WellFormed := by decide
   106	
   107	/-- A profile whose pointers have no room for its addresses is not: 32-bit addresses in a 2-byte
   108	pointer. -/
   109	example : ¬ ({ linuxX8664 with
   110	    addrBits := 32
   111	    ptrSize := 2
   112	    ptrAlign := 2
   113	    maxObject := 2 ^ 16 } : Target).WellFormed := by decide
   114	
   115	/-- Nor is one whose pointer alignment is not a power of two. -/
   116	example : ¬ ({ linuxX8664 with ptrAlign := 6 } : Target).WellFormed := by decide
   117	
   118	/-- Nor one whose pointer size is not a multiple of its alignment. -/
   119	example : ¬ ({ linuxX8664 with ptrAlign := 16 } : Target).WellFormed := by decide
   120	
   121	/-- Nor one whose objects are too small to hold a pointer. -/
   122	example : ¬ ({ linuxX8664 with maxObject := 4 } : Target).WellFormed := by decide
   123	
   124	/-- Nor one whose objects can reach the end of the address range, so that one past the end would
   125	not be an address. -/
   126	example : ¬ ({ linuxX8664 with maxObject := 2 ^ 64 } : Target).WellFormed := by decide
   127	
   128	/-- A small target with 16-bit addresses and 16-bit stored lengths is well formed: nothing assumes
   129	64 bits. -/
   130	example : ({ linuxX8664 with
   131	    addrBits := 16
   132	    ptrSize := 2
   133	    ptrAlign := 2
   134	    lenBits := 16
   135	    maxObject := 2 ^ 15 } : Target).WellFormed := by decide
